From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Ask for a capture period the graph divides evenly

The sound a cast sends is taken from the monitor of its own sink, and the
period it is taken in was set to a round forty milliseconds. At the rate
everything here runs that is 1920 samples, while the graph around it runs in
1024, and one is not a multiple of the other: every cycle of the capture
straddles two of the sink's and begins on the boundary of neither.

Nothing fails. Neither node records a single late or missed cycle, and both
spend well under a thousandth of the time they are given. What is left is the
converter between them realigning continuously, which is not counted
anywhere and is heard as a crackle under sound that is otherwise clean.

Two thousand and forty-eight samples is exactly two of the graph's cycles and
two and a half milliseconds longer than what was asked for before, so the
reason the period was lengthened in the first place still stands: it is far
above the ten milliseconds that had the graph running in periods of 256 and
underrunning at both ends.

This asks for a period that divides and does not insist that the graph keep
the size it has. The graph's size is negotiated, and holding it still would
hold it still for everything on the machine - which is too much to take from
somebody's audio work for the sake of a cast.

diff -ruN a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
--- a/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
+++ b/src/nd-pulseaudio.c
@@ -705,15 +705,32 @@
   /* With pulsesrc's default 10 ms period the sound server runs the whole
    * graph around the sink in periods of 256 samples, which neither this
    * source nor the players feeding the sink keep up with: both record
-   * underruns, heard as a stutter on the receiver. A 40 ms period lets the
-   * graph run at the period players already ask for, and costs nothing
-   * next to the latency of the stream itself. */
+   * underruns, heard as a stutter on the receiver. A period of about forty
+   * milliseconds lets the graph run at the period players already ask for,
+   * and costs nothing next to the latency of the stream itself.
+   *
+   * 42667 microseconds rather than a round 40000, because what matters is
+   * not the length of the period but how it divides. At the rate this runs,
+   * forty milliseconds is 1920 samples against a graph of 1024, which is one
+   * and seven eighths: every cycle of this source straddles two of the
+   * sink's and lands on the boundary of neither, so what sits between them
+   * is forever realigning. Nothing fails and nothing is counted as late; it
+   * is heard rather than measured, as a crackle under sound that is
+   * otherwise clean. 2048 samples is exactly two, and two and a half
+   * milliseconds longer than before.
+   *
+   * It holds while the graph runs at 1024, which is what it does here and
+   * what it is not promised to do: another client may negotiate it
+   * elsewhere. Pinning it would mean pinning the graph for everything on the
+   * machine, which is too much to ask of somebody's audio work for the sake
+   * of a cast, so this asks for the right period and does not insist on the
+   * world around it. */
   g_object_set (src,
                 "device", device_name,
                 "client-name", client_name,
                 "do-timestamp", TRUE,
-                "latency-time", (gint64) 40000,
-                "buffer-time", (gint64) 400000,
+                "latency-time", (gint64) 42667,
+                "buffer-time", (gint64) 426670,
                 "server", pa_context_get_server (self->context),
                 NULL);
 
